arXiv:2606.13280v1 [math.ST] 11 Jun 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based 
<br class="ltx_break">Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Insung Kong
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Niklas Dexheimer
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Johannes Schmidt-Hieber
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Department of Applied Mathematics, University of Twente</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A refined statistical understanding of LLM pre-training requires the analysis of the transformer architecture for data distributions that encapsulate key characteristics of text data. To address this, we propose a text data distribution based on an extension of the log-bilinear language model from the natural language processing literature. For this data generating process, we derive generalization bounds for deep transformer architectures, highlighting the dependence on the network architecture, the vocabulary size, the number of documents and the document length.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent progress in statistical learning theory shows that the success of different neural network architectures can be explained by their respective inductive biases. Deep (feedforward) neural networks (DNNs) are naturally studied within the classical supervised learning paradigm and a well-studied inductive bias is the ability of DNNs to learn compositional structure in the target function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. Similarly, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) originate from image classification problems. Statistical theory can reveal distinctive properties of CNNs if studied for a statistical image classification model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer architectures are at the heart of the current AI revolution. Because they are far more complex than standard feedforward networks, their generalization guarantees remain less understood. There is, however, an impressive line of very recent work studying transformers within the standard supervised learning framework and relating them to conventional models, such as kernel-based estimators or DNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we argue that in order to unravel the specific inductive biases of LLM pre-training, a natural next step is to base the transformer analysis on statistical models for text data. Text data occur as a collection of documents, where each document is a sequence of words or tokens. During pre-training, the task is to learn predicting the next word/token in the sequence. This means to learn a conditional probability mass function on a discrete space. The specific structure of text induces constraints on the conditional probabilities. Inspired by the log-bilinear language model proposed in Mnih and Hinton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, we introduce a new statistical model for text data that we call the nonparametric two-factor (N2F) language model. The N2F language model incorporates key characteristics of text by assuming that the conditional probabilities are a product of two (unobserved) factors, where the first factor is universal, and the second factor depends on a latent context function that changes from document to document. Both factors involve unknown functions, which makes the next word/token prediction problem nonparametric. Learning the first function requires aggregating information across all available documents, whereas learning the second factor only relies on the data in an individual document and bears some resemblance with the in-context learning task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify moreover to which extent the inductive bias of the transformer architecture allows learning next word/token prediction in the proposed N2F language model. To this end, we consider fitting a transformer architecture to data generated from the N2F language model, closely following the transformer architectures applied in practice. In particular, the analyzed architecture incorporates word encoding, positional encoding, the standard multi-head self-attention layers with the softmax function, and the possibility to stack different transformer blocks on top of each other. Studying minimization of the cross-entropy loss, our main result (Theorem <a href="#Thmconj21" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is a risk bound (generalization guarantee) for the transformer applied to the proposed statistical model for next-word/token prediction. It reveals the dependence of the risk on the various size parameters of the transformer architecture, the hardness of the underlying learning problem, and two quantities related to the uniformity of the word embeddings. By optimizing the size parameters of the transformer architecture, nonparametric rates for estimating the two functions in the N2F model occur (Corollary <a href="#Thmconj22" title="Corollary 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). As the vocabulary size <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is large, we argue that one has to include it into the asymptotics, revealing an intricate interplay between <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and the convergence rates associated to the function learning problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the generalization bound relies on an oracle inequality that decomposes the overall risk in an approximation error and a stochastic term. While the connection between the self-attention layer and the Nadaraya–Watson estimator has been made earlier for the in-context regression problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, a new technical difficulty of our construction is to
identify a Nadaraya–Watson estimator which can be used to estimate
the second factor in the proposed N2F language model and to prove that the transformer can approximate it. The analysis requires to derive convergence rates for the Nadaraya–Watson estimator under dependent data generated from a Markov chain supported on a discrete state space and under a non-standard smoothness constraint, as we can only guarantee Hölder smoothness of the <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized function, see Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The article is structured as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> discusses relevant aspects of statistical text modeling and introduces the new statistical model for next-word/token prediction. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the transformer class is formally introduced. The assumptions and the risk bounds are given in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> together with an outline of the main steps in the proof. The main contribution is the control of the approximation error using transformers and key steps of the construction are explained in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. All related work is discussed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Related work ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Detailed proofs are deferred to the appendices.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use standard notation for sets, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}\coloneqq\{1,2,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}\coloneqq\{1,2,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}_{0}\coloneqq\{0,1,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}_{0}\coloneqq\{0,1,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]\coloneqq\{1,2,\ldots,n\},\mathbb{B}^{m}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{2}\leq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]\coloneqq\{1,2,\ldots,n\},\mathbb{B}^{m}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{2}\leq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{m-1}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{2}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{m-1}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{2}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{m-1}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in[0,1]^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{1}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{m-1}\coloneq\{\mathbf{x}\in[0,1]^{m},\|\mathbf{x}\|_{1}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for the natural logarithm and define <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\vee y\coloneqq\max(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\vee y\coloneqq\max(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> as well as <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\land y\coloneqq\min(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\land y\coloneqq\min(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Vectors, matrix-valued and vector-valued functions are denoted by bold letters.
We adopt the notation <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{0}_{d}=(0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{0}_{d}=(0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{1}_{d}=(1,\ldots,1)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{1}_{d}=(1,\ldots,1)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unless otherwise specified,
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{j}\coloneq(0,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{j}\coloneq(0,\ldots,1,0,\ldots,0)^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th standard basis vector, and we define <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\ldots,v_{d}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\ldots,v_{d}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{i})_{i\in[d]}\coloneqq(v_{1},\ldots,v_{d})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{i})_{i\in[d]}\coloneqq(v_{1},\ldots,v_{d})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of a vector <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For column vectors <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the stacked column vector is <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{stack}(\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m})\coloneqq(\mathbf{v}_{1}^{\top},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}^{\top})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>stack</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{stack}(\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m})\coloneqq(\mathbf{v}_{1}^{\top},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}^{\top})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its length is the sum of the lengths of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Column-wise concatenation yields the matrix
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{v}_{i})_{i\in[m]}\coloneqq[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{v}_{i})_{i\in[m]}\coloneqq[\mathbf{v}_{1},\mathbf{v}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}]</annotation></semantics></math> whose columns are <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (assuming they are all of the same length).
For a matrix <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote its <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th column by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{:,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{:,t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For vector or matrix-valued functions <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{f}\|_{L^{\infty}(D)}\coloneq\sup_{\mathbf{x}\in D}\|\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x})\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{f}\|_{L^{\infty}(D)}\coloneq\sup_{\mathbf{x}\in D}\|\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x})\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum entry vector/matrix norm.
We write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\lesssim g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\lesssim g(n)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\leq Cg(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\leq Cg(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\asymp g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\asymp g(n)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\lesssim g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\lesssim g(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\gtrsim g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\gtrsim g(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, we write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)=\tilde{O}(g(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)=\tilde{O}(g(n))</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\leq Cg(n)\log^{k}g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\leq Cg(n)\log^{k}g(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)=\tilde{\Omega}(g(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)=\tilde{\Omega}(g(n))</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\geq Cg(n)\log^{-k}g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\geq Cg(n)\log^{-k}g(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)/g(n)\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)/g(n)\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(n)\ll g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(n)\ll g(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For real numbers <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, we write
<math id="S1.SS1.p1.m47" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{t\in[a,b]}\coloneqq\sum_{t\in([a,b]\cap\mathbb{Z})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.111em" rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t\in[a,b]}\coloneqq\sum_{t\in([a,b]\cap\mathbb{Z})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m48" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bullet_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector with unspecified entries.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Statistical modeling of next-token prediction</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical next word prediction models the conditional probability distribution of the next word given a sequence of words. The string <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘Yesterday, the weather’</span> should very likely be continued with <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">’was’</span>. In this case, the last words contain all the information for predicting the next word. However, to continue <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘Yesterday, the weather was’</span> might be continued by <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘nice’</span> or <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘bad’</span>. Without any further context, the next word has to be sampled from a distribution of all possible continuations. The string <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘We went to a nearby bar. We met some friends that we didn’t see in a long time and ordered a …’</span> should be continued with <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘beer’, ‘cocktail’, ‘glass of wine’, …</span>. If one only includes the last words for the prediction of the next word, one would have forgotten that the scene happens in a bar, and the string could also be continued by <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘three course menu’</span>. From a statistical modeling perspective, one can either assume that there is a form of long-range dependence in documents that causes certain words (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘bar’</span>) to become relevant for the next word prediction much later in documents. Alternatively, one can assume that each document is associated with a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">latent context</span> that induces a different conditional probability distribution for the next word given the previous words. In the example above, the context could be ‘friends in a bar’, which would then put more mass on words such as <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘beer’, ‘wine’, …</span> in the next word prediction problem above. While long-memory can be incorporated via a frequentist model, we could also imagine a document to be generated hierarchically by first drawing a latent context and then sampling tokens conditionally on the context. The randomly sampled context gives this modeling approach a Bayesian flavor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we adopt the latent context modeling approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>.
Let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneqq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">denote the set of token candidates, where each token is represented by a <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional standard basis vector.
To generate a document, a context function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}:(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}:(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is first drawn from some distribution.
Given <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is the next token <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> following the tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\Big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{f},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\Big)\propto\exp\Big(\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big),\qquad w\in[W]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\Big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{f},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\Big)\propto\exp\Big(\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big),\qquad w\in[W].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The goal is to estimate the conditional distribution of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{T+1}|\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{T+1}|\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> given a collection of documents.
This requires estimating the transition probabilities of a discrete Markov chain of order <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
Classical methods include the <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\big(w|\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big)\coloneq\frac{\Big|\Big\{t\in\{r,\ldots,T-1\}:(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t},\mathbf{X}_{t+1})=(\mathbf{X}_{T-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T},\mathbf{e}_{w})\Big\}\Big|}{\Big|\Big\{t\in\{r,\ldots,T-1\}:(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})=(\mathbf{X}_{T-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big\}\Big|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\big(w|\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big)\coloneq\frac{\Big|\Big\{t\in\{r,\ldots,T-1\}:(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t},\mathbf{X}_{t+1})=(\mathbf{X}_{T-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T},\mathbf{e}_{w})\Big\}\Big|}{\Big|\Big\{t\in\{r,\ldots,T-1\}:(\mathbf{X}_{t-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})=(\mathbf{X}_{T-r+1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big\}\Big|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">(with tuning parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1,2,\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1,2,\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>) and Kneser–Ney smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
Each r-gram <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{e}_{w_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{w_{r}})\in(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{e}_{w_{1}},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{w_{r}})\in(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to appear <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T/W^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T/W^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> times on average in a document of length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> such that, without imposing any structural constraint, the convergence rate is <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gtrsim W^{r}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≳</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gtrsim W^{r}/T</annotation></semantics></math> (see Theorem 5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> for the stronger lower bound <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Omega}(W^{r+1}/T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Omega}(W^{r+1}/T)</annotation></semantics></math>). The vocabulary size <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in large language models is typically in the range <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, and cannot be regarded as negligible. However, these arguments ignore that the transition probabilities are highly structured objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Without assumption on the context function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, we cannot achieve a better convergence rate than a <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator,
even when learning a model (e.g., a transformer) from a training dataset composed of multiple documents, as each document is associated with a different context function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this reason, we introduce assumptions on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> through (i) token embeddings and (ii) a decomposition into a linguistic function and a context function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding of tokens into the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Euclidean space should map semantically similar words to nearby points in the embedding space, causing
the conditional probabilities in the language model to take similar values.
Although words form a discrete subset of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{m},</annotation></semantics></math> this representation allows us to impose smoothness. A key example is the log-bilinear language model by Mnih and Hinton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, assuming</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)\propto\exp\Bigg(\bm{\ell}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\Bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)\propto\exp\Bigg(\bm{\ell}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\Bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding, and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\ell}_{0}:\mathbb{R}^{m\times t}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\ell}_{0}:\mathbb{R}^{m\times t}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is an affine map shared across all documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Although we model latent context via a random score function <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Background ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), one may expect that score functions across different documents share common basic linguistic structure, since their short-range dependencies are largely invariant across documents.
For example, <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘Yesterday, the weather was’</span> is likely to be followed by <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘nice’</span> or <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘bad’</span> but not by <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘cat’</span> or <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘attacked’</span>, regardless of the overall context of a document.
Motivated by this intuition, we decompose the score function into a linguistic function and a context function,
which capture short-range dependencies and
latent context, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Data generating process</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As transformers are commonly applied to tokens, we refer to the states as tokens. Tokens are coded as standard basis vectors and are therefore elements of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{W}\coloneq\{\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
For fixed but unknown <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{2}\in\{2,3,\ldots,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2}\in\{2,3,\ldots,\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we assume that there exist unknown token embeddings</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\phi}_{1}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow[-1,1]^{m_{1}}\quad\text{and}\quad\bm{\phi}_{2}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\phi}_{1}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow[-1,1]^{m_{1}}\quad\text{and}\quad\bm{\phi}_{2}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">mapping the tokens <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{e}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> to the hypercube <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]^{m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]^{m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the unit sphere <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math> We call
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linguistic embedding</span>,
and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context embedding</span>.
To facilitate the analysis, we will impose in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmconj1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> regularity conditions ensuring that the context embedding vectors <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})</annotation></semantics></math> are (almost) uniformly located on the unit sphere <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we assume that the information of the last <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens can be mapped to a cube through a</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{{universal} linguistic function}\quad\ \mathbf{g}_{0}:[-1,1]^{m_{1}r_{1}}\rightarrow[-\delta_{1},\delta_{1}]^{m_{1}},\quad\text{for some}\ \ \delta_{1}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">universal</mtext><mtext> linguistic function</mtext></mrow><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for some</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{{universal} linguistic function}\quad\ \mathbf{g}_{0}:[-1,1]^{m_{1}r_{1}}\rightarrow[-\delta_{1},\delta_{1}]^{m_{1}},\quad\text{for some}\ \ \delta_{1}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To account for varying contexts across texts, we further assume that each token is associated with
a</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{{local} context function}\quad\ \mathbf{h}:[-1,1]^{m_{2}r_{2}}\rightarrow\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1},\quad\text{for some}\ \ \delta_{2}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">local</mtext><mtext> context function</mtext></mrow><mspace style="width:1.5em;" width="1.5em"></mspace><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for some</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{{local} context function}\quad\ \mathbf{h}:[-1,1]^{m_{2}r_{2}}\rightarrow\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1},\quad\text{for some}\ \ \delta_{2}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Throughout the paper, we do not track the dependence on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and treat them as constants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">To generate a document, a context function <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is first drawn from a distribution supported on a function class <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\subset\{\mathbf{h}:\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}r_{2}}\rightarrow\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\subset\{\mathbf{h}:\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}r_{2}}\rightarrow\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that the probability that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is the next token <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> following the tokens <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\bigg(\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\bigg)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\bigg(\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">setting
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{s})\coloneq\bm{0}_{m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{s})\coloneq\bm{0}_{m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{s})\coloneq\bm{0}_{m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{s})\coloneq\bm{0}_{m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to this as the <span id="S2.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nonparametric two-factor (N2F) language model.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The function representation is, in general, non-unique, as there could be different pairs <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{g}_{0},\mathbf{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{g}_{0},\mathbf{h})</annotation></semantics></math> leading to the same probability distribution. While the model captures characteristics of text data, it is not intended as a model to generate text. For instance, the next-token distribution is typically supported on a sparse subset of all tokens, giving rise to negligible probabilities for most tokens. To incorporate this in the N2F model would require that the right hand side in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) can change by orders of magnitude,
violating the boundedness constraints <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\infty}\leq\delta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\infty}\leq\delta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{h}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{h}\|_{2}\leq\delta_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Instead, the N2F language model should be considered as a starting point for a statistical learning type analysis of text data focusing on the ability of transformers to quickly learn the context from individual documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The training data consist of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independent training documents of length <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T+1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\big(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\big),\quad i=1,\ldots,n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\big),\quad i=1,\ldots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, each of the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> documents is generated by first independently sampling a context function <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then recursively generating tokens from the probabilistic model (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). In practice, the task is to always use the previous tokens to predict the next token for the whole document. For the theory, we only do this for the last token. Specifically, the considered task is to predict the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st token distribution given the first <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, where the predictor <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{0}\coloneq\{\mathbf{p}:(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T}\rightarrow\Delta^{W-1}\},\ \ \text{with}\ \ (\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T}\coloneq\underbrace{\mathcal{E}_{W}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{E}_{W}}_{T\ \text{times}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>≔</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.410em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>times</mtext></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{0}\coloneq\{\mathbf{p}:(\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T}\rightarrow\Delta^{W-1}\},\ \ \text{with}\ \ (\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T}\coloneq\underbrace{\mathcal{E}_{W}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{E}_{W}}_{T\ \text{times}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">For any (new) document <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1})</annotation></semantics></math> generated from the same distribution as the training samples,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">is an approximation of the true conditional distribution</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\coloneq\bigg(\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{T+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big)\bigg)_{w\in[W]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\coloneq\bigg(\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{T+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big)\bigg)_{w\in[W]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> does not get any direct information of the latent function <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as input, the estimator can learn an approximation <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> from the token sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independently generated test documents</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\big),\quad i=n+1,\ldots,n+N," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\big),\quad i=n+1,\ldots,n+N,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">drawn from the same distribution as the training data, the statistical task it to evaluate how well, in average, the estimator <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> allows us to predict the <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st token from the first <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens.
The corresponding test error (also referred to as <span id="S2.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">log-perplexity</span> in the NLP literature) is given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=n+1}^{n+N}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=n+1}^{n+N}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and using that <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is one-hot encoded, the associated population risk is</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\Big[\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\log\big(\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\Big[\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\log\big(\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\Big[\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\log\big(\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{test}}\Big[\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\log\big(\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.5" class="ltx_p">for an independently sampled
<math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T+1})</annotation></semantics></math>
that has the same distribution as the training documents.
The first term on the right hand side is the expected Kullback–Leibler divergence between the conditional distributions of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second term does not depend on the estimator.
Hence, the first term serves as the excess (population) risk.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The transformer function class</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a mathematical definition of the individual components of the transformer architecture, which then leads to the mathematical definition of the transformer function class at the end of the section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Softmax)<span id="S3.Thmconj1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a vector <math id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{d})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{d})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, the softmax function is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{x})\coloneq\Big(\frac{\exp(x_{1})}{\sum_{i=1}^{d}\exp(x_{i})},\ldots,\frac{\exp(x_{d})}{\sum_{i=1}^{d}\exp(x_{i})}\Big)^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{softmax}(\mathbf{x})\coloneq\Big(\frac{\exp(x_{1})}{\sum_{i=1}^{d}\exp(x_{i})},\ldots,\frac{\exp(x_{d})}{\sum_{i=1}^{d}\exp(x_{i})}\Big)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the input is a <math id="S3.Thmconj1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times T</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, the softmax function is applied row-wise,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{softmax}\big((x_{i,t})_{i,t}\big)\coloneq\bigg(\frac{\exp(x_{i,t})}{\sum_{t=1}^{T}\exp(x_{i,t})}\bigg)_{i,t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{softmax}\big((x_{i,t})_{i,t}\big)\coloneq\bigg(\frac{\exp(x_{i,t})}{\sum_{t=1}^{T}\exp(x_{i,t})}\bigg)_{i,t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Self-attention)<span id="S3.Thmconj2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a dimension <math id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and learnable matrices <math id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U,V\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U,V\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the output of a self-attention layer with input
<math id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)\coloneq VX\operatorname{softmax}\big(X^{\top}UX\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SA</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)\coloneq VX\operatorname{softmax}\big(X^{\top}UX\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmconj2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the class of self-attention layers with bounded parameters</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{SA}(N,B)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}:U,V\in[-B,B]^{N\times N}\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>SA</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{SA}(N,B)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}:U,V\in[-B,B]^{N\times N}\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a matrix input <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\coloneq(\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\coloneq(\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SA</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)_{:,t}=\sum_{s=1}^{T}\frac{\exp(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{s}U\mathbf{x}_{t})}{\sum_{r=1}^{T}\exp(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{r}U\mathbf{x}_{t})}V\mathbf{x}_{s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SA</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{SA}_{U,V}(X)_{:,t}=\sum_{s=1}^{T}\frac{\exp(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{s}U\mathbf{x}_{t})}{\sum_{r=1}^{T}\exp(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{r}U\mathbf{x}_{t})}V\mathbf{x}_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The original definition of the self-attention layer further factorizes the matrix <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\top}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\top}Q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> the so-called key matrix and <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> the so-called query matrix. For notational simplicity, we directly work here with the matrix product.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Multi-head self-attention)<span id="S3.Thmconj3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a dimension <math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, number of heads <math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in[-B,B]^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in[-B,B]^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}\in\mathcal{SA}(N,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}\in\mathcal{SA}(N,B)</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of a multi-head self-attention layer with input
<math id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MA}_{f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}}(X)\coloneq X+\sum_{h=1}^{H}f_{h}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>MA</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MA}_{f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}}(X)\coloneq X+\sum_{h=1}^{H}f_{h}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The class of multi-head self-attention layers with bounded parameters is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{MA}_{f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}}:f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}\in\mathcal{SA}(N,B)\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>MA</mi><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{MA}_{f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}}:f_{1},\ldots,f_{H}\in\mathcal{SA}(N,B)\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The function class <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(N^{2}+N^{2})=2HN^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(N^{2}+N^{2})=2HN^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> many parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Multilayer perceptron)<span id="S3.Thmconj4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given input and output dimensions <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{in}},d_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{in}},d_{\mathrm{out}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, depth <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a multilayer perceptron parameterized by <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math> hidden layers, <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math> neurons in each hidden layer and the ReLU activation function <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto x\vee 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto x\vee 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{MLP}\big(d_{\mathrm{in}},d_{\mathrm{out}},L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}}\Big|\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}\text{has depth }L_{\mathrm{mlp}},\text{width }N_{\mathrm{mlp}},\text{and }\|\bm{\theta}\|_{\infty}\leq B\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>in</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>out</mi></msub></msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">has depth </mtext><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">width </mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MLP}\big(d_{\mathrm{in}},d_{\mathrm{out}},L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{in}}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{out}}}\Big|\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}\text{has depth }L_{\mathrm{mlp}},\text{width }N_{\mathrm{mlp}},\text{and }\|\bm{\theta}\|_{\infty}\leq B\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For sequential input <math id="S3.Thmconj4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\coloneq(\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\coloneq(\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output of the multilayer perceptron is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(X)\coloneq\Big(\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{1}),\ldots,\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{T})\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>MLP</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(X)\coloneq\Big(\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{1}),\ldots,\operatorname{MLP}_{\bm{\theta}}(\mathbf{x}_{T})\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The formal definition of multilayer perceptrons is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Construction of multilayer perceptron layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.
For simplicity, we omit skip connections in the MLP layers. However, our results can be readily extended to models with skip connections (e.g., see Lemma B.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer block)<span id="S3.Thmconj5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmconj5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmconj5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the class of transformer blocks</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\coloneq\Big\{\mathbf{h}\circ\mathbf{g}:\mathbf{g}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B),\,\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\coloneq\Big\{\mathbf{h}\circ\mathbf{g}:\mathbf{g}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B),\,\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-head self-attention has <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2HN^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2HN^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> learnable parameters and the multilayer perceptron has <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N+1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N+1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)N</annotation></semantics></math> many learnable parameters.
Hence, the number of parameters of the transformer block is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2HN^{2}+2NN_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+N." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2HN^{2}+2NN_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For an input matrix</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\coloneq\big[\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T}\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times T}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\coloneq\big[\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{T}\big]\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">we consider a transformer model <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks, model dimension <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, attention layers with <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads, multilayer perceptrons with depth <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and parameter range <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
The output of this model can be represented as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{f}(X)=\Big(D\cdot\operatorname{TB}_{L}\circ\cdots\circ\operatorname{TB}_{1}(P+EX)\Big)_{:,T}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>TB</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>TB</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{f}(X)=\Big(D\cdot\operatorname{TB}_{L}\circ\cdots\circ\operatorname{TB}_{1}(P+EX)\Big)_{:,T}\in\mathbb{R}^{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[L]</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TB}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>TB</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TB}_{\ell}\in\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> is a transformer block, composed with a multi-head self-attention with <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads and a multilayer perceptron with depth <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in[-B,B]^{N\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in[-B,B]^{N\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in[-B,B]^{W\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in[-B,B]^{W\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable encoding and decoding matrices, respectively. The positional encoding matrix
<math id="S3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is not learnable.
Following the original transformer paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>,
we use the following multi-scale sinusoidal positional encoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Positional encoding)<span id="S3.Thmconj6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leq N/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leq N/2</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the positional encoding matrix <math id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{0}_{N-2A}&amp;\ldots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-2A}\\
\mathbf{q}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{q}_{T}\end{matrix}\right],\quad\mathbf{q}_{t}\coloneq\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{q}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{q}_{2,t}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{q}_{A,t}\end{pmatrix},\quad\mathbf{q}_{a,t}\coloneq\left(\begin{matrix}\sin\left(\frac{t}{T^{a/A}}\right)\\
\cos\left(\frac{t}{T^{a/A}}\right)\end{matrix}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{0}_{N-2A}&amp;\ldots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-2A}\\
\mathbf{q}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{q}_{T}\end{matrix}\right],\quad\mathbf{q}_{t}\coloneq\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{q}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{q}_{2,t}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{q}_{A,t}\end{pmatrix},\quad\mathbf{q}_{a,t}\coloneq\left(\begin{matrix}\sin\left(\frac{t}{T^{a/A}}\right)\\
\cos\left(\frac{t}{T^{a/A}}\right)\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Throughout the paper, we assume that <math id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S3.Thmconj6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\coloneq N/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\coloneq N/4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13280v1/positional_encoding.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/130;" width="476" height="130" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Illustration of the multi-scale sinusoidal positional encoding defined in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmconj6" title="Definition 3.6 (Positional encoding). ‣ 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> for <math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=20</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S3.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in[A]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in[A]</annotation></semantics></math>,
the vectors <math id="S3.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{a,t}=(\sin(t/T^{a/A}),\cos(t/T^{a/A}))^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{a,t}=(\sin(t/T^{a/A}),\cos(t/T^{a/A}))^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, lie on the unit circle, with smaller <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to faster rotation.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows an illustration of the positional encoding defined above.
The positional encoding is necessary in the transformer architecture because all other operators (multi-head self-attention, multilayer perceptron, encoder and decoder) are permutation invariant<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is permutation invariant if <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\Pi X)=\Pi f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\Pi X)=\Pi f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for any column-wise permutation operator <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>.
Most existing works on the statistical analysis of transformers employ the single-scale sinusoidal positional encoding (i.e., <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=1</annotation></semantics></math>). For our analysis, it is crucial to use instead the multi-scale sinusoidal positional encoding with a sufficiently large <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this reduces the magnitude of the parameters from polynomial order in <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to logarithmic order when approximating the hardmax function by the softmax function in the self-attention layer, see Remark <a href="#Thmrem2" title="Remark 2. ‣ E.1 Construction of multi-head self-attention layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In the definition of <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{q,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{q,t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced by any <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}\geq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}\geq T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although this formulation is more natural, we take <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\max}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\max}=T</annotation></semantics></math> for notational simplicity. We now give the final definition of the transformer function class used in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmconj7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmconj7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmconj7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Transformer function class)<span id="S3.Thmconj7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmconj7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\mathbf{f}:(\mathbb{R}^{W})^{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{W}\text{ has the form \eqref{eq_def_transformer} with }E,D^{\top}\in[-B,B]^{N\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> has the form (</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">) with </mtext></mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\mathbf{f}:(\mathbb{R}^{W})^{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{W}\text{ has the form \eqref{eq_def_transformer} with }E,D^{\top}\in[-B,B]^{N\times W}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ and }\operatorname{TB}_{1},\ldots,\operatorname{TB}_{L}\in\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>TB</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>TB</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ and }\operatorname{TB}_{1},\ldots,\operatorname{TB}_{L}\in\mathcal{TB}(N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In addition, we define the class of clipped transformer
networks for <math id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{M}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{clip}_{M}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>clip</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{M}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\coloneq\Big\{\operatorname{clip}_{M}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmconj7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{clip}_{M}:x\mapsto(-M\vee x)\land M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>clip</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{clip}_{M}:x\mapsto(-M\vee x)\land M</annotation></semantics></math>
is applied element-wise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), the number of learnable parameters in the (clipped) transformer class is</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2WN+L\Big(2HN^{2}+2NN_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+N\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2WN+L\Big(2HN^{2}+2NN_{\mathrm{mlp}}+(L_{\mathrm{mlp}}-1)N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+N\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Risk bound</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Regularity conditions in fixed design settings are commonly imposed in nonparametric statistics (e.g., see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>). As mentioned in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we require the context embedding vectors <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})</annotation></semantics></math> to be regularly spaced on the unit sphere.
The regularity is measured by the function <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and the quantity <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{W}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmconj1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmconj1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmconj1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmconj1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{W}:(0,\infty)\rightarrow(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{W}:(0,\infty)\rightarrow(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{W}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{W}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)\coloneqq\inf_{w\in[W]}\frac{1}{W}\Big|\big\{{w^{\prime}}\in[W]:\|\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})-\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w^{\prime}})\|_{2}\leq\epsilon\big\}\Big|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>W</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)\coloneqq\inf_{w\in[W]}\frac{1}{W}\Big|\big\{{w^{\prime}}\in[W]:\|\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})-\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w^{\prime}})\|_{2}\leq\epsilon\big\}\Big|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmconj1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{W}\coloneq\sup_{\mathbf{v}\in\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\left\|\frac{\sum_{w=1}^{W}\exp\big(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)\big)\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)}{\sum_{w=1}^{W}\exp\big(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)\big)}-\frac{\int_{\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\exp(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{z})\mathbf{z}d\mathbf{z}}{\int_{\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\exp(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z}}\right\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{W}\coloneq\sup_{\mathbf{v}\in\delta_{2}\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\left\|\frac{\sum_{w=1}^{W}\exp\big(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)\big)\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)}{\sum_{w=1}^{W}\exp\big(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(w)\big)}-\frac{\int_{\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\exp(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{z})\mathbf{z}d\mathbf{z}}{\int_{\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}}\exp(\mathbf{v}^{\top}\mathbf{z})d\mathbf{z}}\right\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)\geq 1/W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)\geq 1/W</annotation></semantics></math> holds for any <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, if <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})</annotation></semantics></math> are independent and identically distributed samples from the uniform distribution on <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{2}\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{2}\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then a covering number argument yields the following assumption with high probability; see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Discussion about Assumption ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for detail.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumpt1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumpt">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumpt1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumpt1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumpt1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumpt1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumpt1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmassumpt1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="Thmassumpt1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{-\frac{1}{m_{2}-1}}\log W\leq\epsilon\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{-\frac{1}{m_{2}-1}}\log W\leq\epsilon\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)=\tilde{\Omega}(\epsilon^{m_{2}-1})\quad\text{ and }\quad\kappa_{W}=\tilde{O}(W^{-1/2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varphi_{W}(\epsilon)=\tilde{\Omega}(\epsilon^{m_{2}-1})\quad\text{ and }\quad\kappa_{W}=\tilde{O}(W^{-1/2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumpt1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumpt1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmassumpt1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Omega},\tilde{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Omega},\tilde{O}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the lower/upper bounds for the rate up to log-factors (see section on notation for precise definitions).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The token probabilities following the sequences <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘I am very glad to’</span> and <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘I feel really happy to’</span> should be similar.
To this end, we impose the Hölder smoothness assumption on the linguistic function <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the context function <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This guarantees that if <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{s})\approx\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{s})\approx\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-r_{1}+1\leq s\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-r_{1}+1\leq s\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S4.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\big)\approx\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\big)\approx\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and the same holds for <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmconj2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmconj2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmconj2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S4.Thmconj2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder smooth functions)<span id="S4.Thmconj2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmconj2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=q+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=q+s</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{N}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{N}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder norm of a function <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}=(f_{1},\ldots,f_{d})^{\top}:[-1,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}=(f_{1},\ldots,f_{d})^{\top}:[-1,1]^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\mathbf{f}\right\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta}}\coloneqq\max_{j\in[d^{\prime}]}\bigg(\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\bm{\alpha}\in\mathbb{N}_{0}^{d}\\
\bm{\alpha}:\|\bm{\alpha}\|_{1}\leq q\end{subarray}}\left\|\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}\right\|_{\infty}+\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\bm{\alpha}\in\mathbb{N}_{0}^{d}\\
\bm{\alpha}:\|\bm{\alpha}\|_{1}=q\end{subarray}}\sup_{\underset{\mathbf{x}_{1}\neq\mathbf{x}_{2}}{\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2}\in[-1,1]^{d}}}\frac{\left|\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{1})-\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{2})\right|}{\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|_{\infty}^{s}}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></msub><mo>≔</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝜶</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">≠</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝜶</mi></msup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\mathbf{f}\right\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta}}\coloneqq\max_{j\in[d^{\prime}]}\bigg(\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\bm{\alpha}\in\mathbb{N}_{0}^{d}\\
\bm{\alpha}:\|\bm{\alpha}\|_{1}\leq q\end{subarray}}\left\|\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}\right\|_{\infty}+\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}\bm{\alpha}\in\mathbb{N}_{0}^{d}\\
\bm{\alpha}:\|\bm{\alpha}\|_{1}=q\end{subarray}}\sup_{\underset{\mathbf{x}_{1}\neq\mathbf{x}_{2}}{\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2}\in[-1,1]^{d}}}\frac{\left|\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{1})-\partial^{\bm{\alpha}}f_{j}(\mathbf{x}_{2})\right|}{\|\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}\|_{\infty}^{s}}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that a function <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder smooth if <math id="S4.Thmconj2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{f}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{f}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists and is finite.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumpt2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumpt">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumpt2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumpt2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumpt2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumpt2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumpt2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i) <math id="Thmassumpt2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmassumpt2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(ii) <math id="Thmassumpt2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmassumpt2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Any function on a finite discrete domain can be extended to a Hölder-smooth function on a continuous domain with finite Hölder norm.
Therefore, the Hölder smoothness assumption is only meaningful on a discrete domain if one considers joint asymptotics where also the vocabulary size <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For a class of candidate functions <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\subset\mathcal{P}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\subset\mathcal{P}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the empirical risk minimizer <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the cross-entropy loss, that is,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\in\argmin_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\,-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\in\argmin_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\,-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the logarithm is applied componentwise. As we control the stochastic fluctuation using the multiclass classification oracle inequality in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, one could moreover incorporate the optimization error in the analysis. For notational simplicity, we only consider the empirical risk minimizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we consider</p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{P}=\Big\{\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{T}_{M}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\Big\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{P}=\Big\{\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{T}_{M}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big)\Big\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">as the class of candidate functions in (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).
We further assume that the transformer class is sufficiently large as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumpt3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumpt">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumpt3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumpt3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumpt3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumpt3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmassumpt3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B,M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmassumpt3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L\geq 2,\quad N\geq 8\log T,\quad H\geq r_{1}+r_{2},\quad N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq N\vee T^{\alpha},\quad L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq\log^{3}(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\vee T)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∨</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>∨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L\geq 2,\quad N\geq 8\log T,\quad H\geq r_{1}+r_{2},\quad N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq N\vee T^{\alpha},\quad L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq\log^{3}(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\vee T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B\geq 9\log^{2}T,\quad M\geq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B\geq 9\log^{2}T,\quad M\geq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumpt3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumpt3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some <math id="Thmassumpt3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We now present the main result of this paper. It provides convergence rates for the expected excess risk (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) of transformer-based next-token prediction models.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmconj21.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumpt3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> hold and that
<math id="Thmconj21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{W}\leq(1+m_{2}/\delta_{2})^{-1}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{W}\leq(1+m_{2}/\delta_{2})^{-1}/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
For sufficiently large <math id="Thmconj21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,W," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,W,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconj21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,
the empirical risk minimizer (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) with the candidate class (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)
satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tilde{O}\bigg(\frac{M(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}+\log B)}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tilde{O}\bigg(\frac{M(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}+\log B)}{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\qquad\,\,+N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\inf_{\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]}\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}\Big)+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\qquad\,\,+N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\inf_{\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]}\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}\Big)+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation is taken over all randomness (training data and <math id="Thmconj21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>).
The implicit
constant depends only on <math id="Thmconj21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The first line on the right-hand side represents the stochastic error.
This term increases with the model size but can be controlled by taking a sufficiently large sample size <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., by using more training documents.
The second line corresponds to the approximation error.
In particular, the first term <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-4\beta_{1}/(m_{1}r_{1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>mlp</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-4\beta_{1}/(m_{1}r_{1})}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to approximating the universal linguistic function <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be controlled by increasing the width of the transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The remaining terms of the error bound originate from the learning of the context function <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is random, it cannot be directly used in constructing the transformer for approximation and must instead be estimated from the data.
In the proof, we construct a Nadaraya–Watson estimator with bandwidth <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> whose direction converges to that of <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The term involving the infimum arises from the bias-variance trade-off of the estimator and decreases as <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
Finally, the <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> term appears due to the discreteness of the token embeddings and is typically negligible under mild assumptions (e.g., Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). We want to stress again, that as we apply the risk bound in Theorem 3.5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, one can go beyond the analysis of the empirical risk minimizer at the cost of an additional optimization error term in the rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following corollary follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmconj21" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by additionally imposing Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and selecting an appropriate range of transformer architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj22.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmconj22.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumpt1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumpt3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and further assume that there exists <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max(L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},B,M)\lesssim\log^{k}(n\vee W\vee T)\quad\text{and}\quad N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\asymp n^{\frac{m_{1}r_{1}}{2(2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1})}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>≍</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max(L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},B,M)\lesssim\log^{k}(n\vee W\vee T)\quad\text{and}\quad N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\asymp n^{\frac{m_{1}r_{1}}{2(2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1})}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj22.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj22.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For sufficiently large <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,W," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,W,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq\log W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq\log W</annotation></semantics></math>,
the empirical risk minimizer (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) with the candidate class (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)
satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tilde{O}\Big(\frac{W}{n}+n^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1}}}+T^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{2\beta_{2}+(m_{2}-1)r_{2}}}+W^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{m_{2}-1}}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tilde{O}\Big(\frac{W}{n}+n^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1}}}+T^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{2\beta_{2}+(m_{2}-1)r_{2}}}+W^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{m_{2}-1}}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj22.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj22.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation is taken over all randomness (training data and <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>).
The implicit
constant depends only on <math id="Thmconj22.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmconj21" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the choices in (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In Corollary 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), using Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
and by choosing</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=T^{-\frac{1}{2\beta_{2}+r_{2}(m_{2}-1)}}\vee W^{-\frac{1}{m_{2}-1}}\log W." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>∨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=T^{-\frac{1}{2\beta_{2}+r_{2}(m_{2}-1)}}\vee W^{-\frac{1}{m_{2}-1}}\log W.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The error term <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W/n</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to learning the word embeddings <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}:\mathcal{E}_{W}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It scales linearly in the vocabulary size <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and can be effectively controlled by using a sufficiently large training set.
The error term <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{2\beta_{1}+m_{1}r_{1}}}</annotation></semantics></math> relates to learning the <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder smooth and <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1}r_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1}r_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-variate universal linguistic function <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math> This is the standard nonparametric convergence rate for sample size <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n.</annotation></semantics></math> The error term <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{2\beta_{2}+(m_{2}-1)r_{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{2\beta_{2}+(m_{2}-1)r_{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to learning the <math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder smooth local context function <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> on the domain <math id="S4.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1})^{r_{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{S}^{m_{2}-1})^{r_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math> The convergence rate in the document length <math id="S4.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is surprising as the training loss <math id="S4.p12.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tfrac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{X}^{(i)\top}_{T+1}\log\big(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}^{(i)}_{T})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> considers <math id="S4.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> prediction problems. It remains an open problem, whether the function <math id="S4.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated with a faster rate also involving <math id="S4.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T.</annotation></semantics></math> As <math id="S4.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\gg T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\gg T</annotation></semantics></math> in applications, we view this problem, however, as less relevant. The term <math id="S4.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{m_{2}-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{-\frac{2\beta_{2}}{m_{2}-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> arises in the convergence rate from the discrete structure of the domain, and becomes negligible when <math id="S4.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\gtrsim T^{1/r_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\gtrsim T^{1/r_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We emphasize again that for small (and fixed) vocabulary size <math id="S4.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, classical methods such as the
<math id="S4.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator or Kneser–Ney smoothing remain optimal, without smoothness assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">To circumvent the curse of dimensionality in learning <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can additionally assume that <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is compositionally sparse or is supported on a low-dimensional manifold <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset[-1,1]^{m_{1}r_{1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset[-1,1]^{m_{1}r_{1}}.</annotation></semantics></math> In the proof, one has to replace then the approximation theoretic result for Hölder functions in Lemma <a href="#Thmconj219" title="Lemma 19 (Theorem 1 of []). ‣ E.2 Construction of multilayer perceptron layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> by the respective results for compositionally sparse functions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or functions supported on low-dimensional manifolds in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, it is of interest whether faster rates can be achieved for the estimation of the context function <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}.</annotation></semantics></math> A key step in the proof is to exploit that the transformer architecture can represent a Nadaraya–Watson estimator. The Hölder smoothness <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> of the regression function improves the convergence rate of the Nadaraya–Watson estimator for <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. Chapter 1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>). For the context function <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, the smoothness index is denoted by <math id="S4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is imposed in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (ii). While the transformer can represent the Nadaraya–Watson estimator for a specific kernel, it remains unclear whether the transformer can also represent higher-order local polynomial estimators. If possible, this would allow to exploit higher-order smoothness of <math id="S4.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> beyond Lipschitz continuity (<math id="S4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>). If the data are supported on a low-dimensional manifold <math id="S4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the convergence rate of the Nadaraya–Watson estimator can adapt to the dimension of <math id="S4.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. We conjecture that this phenomenon allows the transformer to circumvent the curse of dimensionality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We derive the generalization bound for specific transformer architectures satisfying the constraints in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In particular, we assume that the number of layers in the MLPs satisfies <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq\log^{3}(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\vee T)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>∨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq\log^{3}(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\vee T).</annotation></semantics></math> One can also obtain a similar generalization bound for shallow networks <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{mlp}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{mlp}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> by increasing the number of transformer blocks <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> instead. To see this, observe that
if the multi-head self-attention represents the identity map, the transformer block coincides with the MLP. If each MLP is shallow, then, stacking <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks represents an MLP of depth <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, one can derive generalization bounds for models with only self-attention layers by following the recipe for translating ReLU approximation results to the softmax attention mechanism in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Proof of the risk bound</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide a proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmconj21" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Notice that the learning task is an i.i.d. multiclass classification problem with inputs <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}^{(i)})\in\mathcal{E}_{W}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{X}_{1}^{(i)},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}^{(i)})\in\mathcal{E}_{W}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding output <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\in\mathcal{E}_{W}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{(i)}\in\mathcal{E}_{W},</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Excess risk bounds for binary classification have been studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> and for multiclass classification in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>.
For the proof, we use the oracle inequality in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that the VC dimension of self-attention layers involving exponential operations is difficult to control, which leads to suboptimal rates in regression problems (see, e.g., the discussion following Theorem 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>).
For this reason, we instead bound the parameter magnitudes and derive an upper bound for the metric entropy of the transformer class <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
The proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proofs for the Stochastic Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
Similar results on the covering numbers of transformers can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for linear attention, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> for ReLU attention, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> for softmax attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj23.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmconj23.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmconj23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconj23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mlp}}\geq N</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="Thmconj23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\delta,\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\delta,\mathcal{T}\big(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B\big),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\Big(2WN+4HLN^{2}+3LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}\Big)\log\bigg(3+\frac{(6H)^{10L^{2}}\big(B(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)\big)^{38L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}}B}{\delta}\bigg)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>38</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\Big(2WN+4HLN^{2}+3LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}\Big)\log\bigg(3+\frac{(6H)^{10L^{2}}\big(B(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}+1)\big)^{38L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}}B}{\delta}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, the bound is independent of the document length <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T.</annotation></semantics></math>
For polynomially small <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and ignoring logarithmic factors, the upper bound in the theorem scales as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2})L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\log B." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2})L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}\log B.</annotation></semantics></math>
The bound in Theorem 5.3 of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> is similar but derived
for finite <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and the log covering number derived in Lemma 4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>
includes an additional <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log T</annotation></semantics></math> factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the proposed statistical model (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and by (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In 2.2 Data generating process ‣ 2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the probabilities in the vector <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> are up to normalization</p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\propto\exp\bigg(\underbrace{\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})}_{\displaystyle\eqcolon\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}\bigg),\quad w\in[W]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn mathsize="1.400em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn mathsize="1.400em">1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi mathsize="1.400em">T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.400em" minsize="1.400em">(</mo><mi mathsize="1.400em">w</mi><mo maxsize="1.400em" minsize="1.400em">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\propto\exp\bigg(\underbrace{\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})}_{\displaystyle\eqcolon\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}\bigg),\quad w\in[W].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The following theorem provides a bound on the approximation error, and serves as a key contribution of this paper.
We provide a proof sketch in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and the formal proof is stated in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proofs for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj24.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4</span></span><span id="Thmconj24.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumpt3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> hold. For sufficiently large <math id="Thmconj24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconj24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="Thmconj24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a transformer <math id="Thmconj24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}\in\mathcal{T}_{M}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}\in\mathcal{T}_{M}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\mathbb{E}\Big[\|\mathbf{f}^{*}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}^{2}\big|\mathbf{h}\Big]=\tilde{O}\bigg(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\mathbb{E}\Big[\|\mathbf{f}^{*}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}^{2}\big|\mathbf{h}\Big]=\tilde{O}\bigg(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj24.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj24.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the implicit
constant depends only on <math id="Thmconj24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}},\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The main difficulty in the construction underlying Theorem <a href="#Thmconj24" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.1 Proof of the risk bound ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> has to be approximated by the data <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Compared to in-context nonparametric regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, the considered statistical model for next-token prediction is significantly more challenging to analyze and requires novel techniques.
In addition, the error in estimating <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> should not deteriorate due to the presence of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which further complicates the analysis.
See Remark <a href="#Thmrem1" title="Remark 1. ‣ 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for further discussion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmconj21" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By assumption <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math> Since</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})=\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})=\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>-th component of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has the form,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}\coloneqq\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}\coloneqq\mathbf{g}_{0}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}\Big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\Big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">we obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}\leq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}\leq\delta_{1}m_{1}+\delta_{2}\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})&lt;\frac{\exp(2M)}{W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})&lt;\frac{\exp(2M)}{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Because of the clipping operation in the transformer function class (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmconj7" title="Definition 3.7 (Transformer function class). ‣ 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>), every <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(-2M)/W&lt;\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-2M)/W&lt;\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}),</annotation></semantics></math>
and hence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\bigg(\frac{\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg)\leq 4M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\bigg(\frac{\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg)\leq 4M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">By using the oracle inequality for multiclass classification
(Theorem 3.5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>),</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\widehat{\mathbf{p}}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex41.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{M}{n}\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\frac{1}{n},\log(\mathcal{P}),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{M}{n}\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\frac{1}{n},\log(\mathcal{P}),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex41.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(stochastic error)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\tilde{O}\bigg(\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\tilde{O}\bigg(\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(approximation error)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(approximation error)</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(approximation error)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">where
<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\mathcal{P})\coloneqq\{\mathbf{f}=\log(\mathbf{p}):\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\mathcal{P})\coloneqq\{\mathbf{f}=\log(\mathbf{p}):\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\mathbf{f}\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\coloneqq\max\{\|\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}:\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\in\mathcal{E}_{W}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≔</mo><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{f}\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\coloneqq\max\{\|\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\|_{\infty}:\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\in\mathcal{E}_{W}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathcal{T}_{M}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\coloneqq\mathcal{T}_{M}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Combining <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\log(\mathcal{P})=\{\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\mathcal{P})=\{\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\,\mathbf{f}:\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequality</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\big\|\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\operatorname{clip}_{M}(\mathbf{x})-\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\operatorname{clip}_{M}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime})\big\|_{\infty}\leq 2\big\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^{\prime}\big\|_{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>clip</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>clip</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em">∥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\|\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\operatorname{clip}_{M}(\mathbf{x})-\log\circ\operatorname{softmax}\circ\operatorname{clip}_{M}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime})\big\|_{\infty}\leq 2\big\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{x}^{\prime}\big\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">and Theorem <a href="#Thmconj23" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 4.1 Proof of the risk bound ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> yield</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{M}{n}\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\frac{1}{n},\log(\mathcal{P}),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)\bigg)=\tilde{O}\Big(\frac{M(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}+\log B)}{n}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>M</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{M}{n}\log\,\mathcal{N}\Big(\frac{1}{n},\log(\mathcal{P}),\|\cdot\|_{L^{\infty}((\mathcal{E}_{W})^{T})}\Big)\bigg)=\tilde{O}\Big(\frac{M(WN+HLN^{2}+LL_{\mathrm{mlp}}N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{2}+L^{2}L_{\mathrm{mlp}}+\log B)}{n}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Using Lemma <a href="#Thmconj232" title="Lemma 32. ‣ Appendix G Technical Lemmas ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>,
the approximation error can be further bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}\log\frac{\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}{\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}\bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\sum_{w=1}^{W}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}\log\frac{\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}{\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})_{(w)}}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 4\inf_{\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}}\,\mathbb{E}\Big[\big\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big\|_{\infty}^{2}\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 4\inf_{\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}}\,\mathbb{E}\Big[\big\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big\|_{\infty}^{2}\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 4\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\,\inf_{\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}}\,\mathbb{E}\Big[\big\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big\|_{\infty}^{2}\,\Big|\,\mathbf{h}\Big]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 4\sup_{\mathbf{h}\in\mathcal{H}}\,\inf_{\mathbf{f}\in\mathcal{F}}\,\mathbb{E}\Big[\big\|\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})-\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})\big\|_{\infty}^{2}\,\Big|\,\mathbf{h}\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.5.1" class="ltx_text">Together with Theorem <a href="#Thmconj24" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.1 Proof of the risk bound ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]=\tilde{O}\bigg(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\inf_{\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]}\,\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}\Big)+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{\mathbf{p}\in\mathcal{P}}\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\frac{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{0}(\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}{\mathbf{X}_{T+1}^{\top}\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T})}\bigg]=\tilde{O}\bigg(N_{\mathrm{mlp}}^{-\frac{4\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\inf_{\lambda\in[T^{-1},\log^{-1}(W\land T)]}\,\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\frac{M^{2}}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}\Big)+\kappa_{W}^{2}\bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.6.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now outline the construction of a transformer <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}\in\mathcal{T}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}\in\mathcal{T}(W,T,L,N,H,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the approximation error bound in Theorem <a href="#Thmconj24" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.1 Proof of the risk bound ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Recall that the input tokens <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are coded as <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional standard basis vectors. The input of the transformer is the <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\times T</annotation></semantics></math> matrix</p>
<table id="S5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\mathbf{X}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{X}_{2}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{X}_{T}\end{matrix}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\mathbf{X}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{X}_{2}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{X}_{T}\end{matrix}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It suffices to describe the construction of an encoder <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, a decoder <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, multi-head self-attentions <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{1},\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{1},\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B),</annotation></semantics></math> and multilayer perceptrons <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}_{1},\bm{\nu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}_{1},\bm{\nu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-th output column of the transformer is</p>
<table id="S5.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{f}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{f}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{clip}_{M}\circ\,\Big(D\cdot\bm{\nu}_{2}\circ\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}(P+EX)\Big)_{:,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>clip</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">∘</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{clip}_{M}\circ\,\Big(D\cdot\bm{\nu}_{2}\circ\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}(P+EX)\Big)_{:,T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},X)\coloneqq\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})\\
\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\end{matrix}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},X)\coloneqq\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})\\
\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{g}_{0}\big(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{1}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})+\mathbf{h}\big(\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T-r_{2}+1}),\ldots,\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\big)^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\end{matrix}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">See Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for an illustration.
The main challenge is that we cannot use the latent local context function <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to construct <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> has itself to be approximated by the available input data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13280v1/overall_structure.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/245;" width="381" height="245" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Main building blocks of the transformer construction to control the approximation error.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the encoder operation, we use the <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times W</annotation></semantics></math> matrix</p>
<table id="S5.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E\coloneq\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For every token <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in[W]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\mathbf{e}_{w}=\operatorname{stack}(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w}),\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w}),\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>stack</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\mathbf{e}_{w}=\operatorname{stack}(\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w}),\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w}),\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}),</annotation></semantics></math> consequently,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EX=E\big[\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big]=\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EX=E\big[\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{T}\big]=\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and combined with the definition of the positional encoding matrix <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S3.Thmconj6" title="Definition 3.6 (Positional encoding). ‣ 3 The transformer function class ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P+EX=\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{q}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{q}_{T}\end{matrix}\right]=\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P+EX=\left[\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{1})&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{2})&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{q}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{q}_{T}\end{matrix}\right]=\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The projected input <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P+EX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P+EX</annotation></semantics></math> is propagated to the first multi-head self attention layer <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{1}m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{1}m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the linguistic and context embeddings of the most recent <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens up to time <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, defined as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}})\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-1})\end{pmatrix},\qquad\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-1})\end{pmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}})\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{1}+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t-1})\end{pmatrix},\qquad\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-r_{2}+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t-1})\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By exploiting the positional encoding, one can construct a multi-head self-attention layer <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{1}\left(\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-(r_{1}+1)m_{1}-(r_{2}+1)m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{1}m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{N/2}\\
\end{array}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mphantom></mphantom></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{1}\left(\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-(r_{1}+1)m_{1}-(r_{2}+1)m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{1}m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{N/2}\\
\end{array}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The approximation is valid for the <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th column for <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq(r_{1}\vee r_{2})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq(r_{1}\vee r_{2})+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Lemma <a href="#Thmconj214" title="Lemma 14. ‣ E.1 Construction of multi-head self-attention layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> for a bound on the approximation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now employ a multilayer perceptron to approximate two key quantities: <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the linguistic information, and</p>
<table id="S5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{t}\coloneqq\frac{\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})}{\exp\big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\big)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{t}\coloneqq\frac{\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})}{\exp\big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\big)}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which will later be used to estimate the local context function <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, we construct a multi-layer perceptron <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.EGx27.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.EGx27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\nu}_{1}\left(\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-(r_{1}+1)m_{1}-(r_{2}+1)m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})\\
\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\
\frac{1}{\lambda}\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\frac{1}{\lambda}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\frac{\sqrt{r_{2}}}{\lambda}\\
T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(t\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-(r_{2}+2)m_{2}-2}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}\\
\in\mathbb{R}\\
\left.\right.\end{array}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><msqrt><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msqrt><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mphantom></mphantom></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\nu}_{1}\left(\left(\begin{matrix}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\mathbf{Z}_{1,t}\\
\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\bm{0}_{\tfrac{N}{2}-(r_{1}+1)m_{1}-(r_{2}+1)m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{q}_{t}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})\\
\mathbf{Y}_{t}\\
\frac{1}{\lambda}\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\\
\frac{1}{\lambda}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{t})\\
\frac{\sqrt{r_{2}}}{\lambda}\\
T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(t\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-(r_{2}+2)m_{2}-2}\end{matrix}\right)_{t\in[T]}\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}\\
\in\mathbb{R}\\
\left.\right.\end{array}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The approximation error is controlled in Lemma <a href="#Thmconj224" title="Lemma 24. ‣ E.2 Construction of multilayer perceptron layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{2}m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S5.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z})\coloneqq\sum_{s\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T]}\frac{K_{\lambda}\big(\mathbf{Z}_{2,s},\mathbf{z}\big)}{\sum_{i\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T]}K_{\lambda}\big(\mathbf{Z}_{2,i},\mathbf{z}\big)}\mathbf{Y}_{s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z})\coloneqq\sum_{s\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T]}\frac{K_{\lambda}\big(\mathbf{Z}_{2,s},\mathbf{z}\big)}{\sum_{i\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T]}K_{\lambda}\big(\mathbf{Z}_{2,i},\mathbf{z}\big)}\mathbf{Y}_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}^{\prime})\coloneq\exp(-\|\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{z}^{\prime}\|^{2}/(2\lambda^{2}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}^{\prime})\coloneq\exp(-\|\mathbf{z}-\mathbf{z}^{\prime}\|^{2}/(2\lambda^{2})).</annotation></semantics></math> The second multi-head self-attention layer <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed to map the right-hand side of (<a href="#S5.EGx27" title="5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to a <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times T</annotation></semantics></math> matrix.
The last column of this matrix is given by
<math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{stack}(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}),\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}},\mathbf{v})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>stack</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{stack}(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}),\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}},\mathbf{v})\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the vector <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{v}\coloneqq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>≔</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{v}\coloneqq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{s=1}^{T}\frac{\exp\Big(\frac{-r_{2}+\mathbf{Z}_{2,s}^{\top}\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}}{\lambda^{2}}+T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(s\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\Big)}{\sum_{i=1}^{T}\exp\Big(\frac{-r_{2}+\mathbf{Z}_{2,i}^{\top}\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}}{\lambda^{2}}+T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(i\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\Big)}\mathbf{Y}_{s}\approx\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{s=1}^{T}\frac{\exp\Big(\frac{-r_{2}+\mathbf{Z}_{2,s}^{\top}\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}}{\lambda^{2}}+T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(s\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\Big)}{\sum_{i=1}^{T}\exp\Big(\frac{-r_{2}+\mathbf{Z}_{2,i}^{\top}\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}}{\lambda^{2}}+T^{3}\,\mathbb{I}(i\in[\log^{2}T,T-\log^{2}T])\Big)}\mathbf{Y}_{s}\approx\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p">This approximation follows from the fact that <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\|_{2}^{2}=r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{Z}_{2,t}\|_{2}^{2}=r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq r_{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq r_{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following lemma characterizes the approximation error of the construction up to this point.
In Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>, we restate the result as Lemma <a href="#Thmconj226" title="Lemma 26 (Restatement of Lemma ). ‣ E.3 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> and provide the formal proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmconj25.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmassumpt3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> hold.
For sufficiently large <math id="Thmconj25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and
for any <math id="Thmconj25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq T^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq T^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist multi-head self-attentions <math id="Thmconj25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{1},\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛍</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝛍</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{1},\bm{\mu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MA}(N,H,B)</annotation></semantics></math> and
a multilayer perceptron <math id="Thmconj25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛎</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}_{1}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.EGx30.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.EGx30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Big(\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}\big(P+EX\big)\Big)_{:,T}=\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\end{array}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mphantom></mphantom></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big(\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}\big(P+EX\big)\Big)_{:,T}=\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj25.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj25.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Big\|\mathbf{k}_{1}-\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\big\|_{\infty}\vee\,\big\|\mathbf{k}_{2}-\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\big\|_{\infty}\lesssim(N_{\mathrm{mlp}})^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\frac{1}{T}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.392em">∨</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big\|\mathbf{k}_{1}-\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\big\|_{\infty}\vee\,\big\|\mathbf{k}_{2}-\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\big\|_{\infty}\lesssim(N_{\mathrm{mlp}})^{-\frac{2\beta_{1}}{m_{1}r_{1}}}+\frac{1}{T},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj25.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj25.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the implicit
constant depends only on <math id="Thmconj25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{1},\|\mathbf{g}_{0}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{1}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now study the properties of (<a href="#S5.E26" title="In 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>).
The estimator
<math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified as a Nadaraya–Watson estimator applied to dependent samples with discrete support.
From the classical nonparametric regression theory (see e.g. Section 1.5 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>), one would expect that <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the conditional expectation <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}]</annotation></semantics></math> under standard smoothness assumptions on the function <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the present setting, however, such convergence cannot be rigorously established, since
<math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]</annotation></semantics></math> can vary considerably as a function of <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see this, recall that for <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution of the tokens is described by the imposed statistical model</p>
<table id="S5.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)\propto\exp\Big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,t+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\Big),\quad w\in[W]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\big(\mathbf{X}_{t+1}=\mathbf{e}_{w}\big|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{X}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{X}_{t}\big)\propto\exp\Big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,t+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{w})+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,t+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{w})\Big),\quad w\in[W].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">One can factorize the conditional expectation in this model via</p>
<table id="S5.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T+1})}{\exp\big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T+1})\big)}\middle|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]=\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{X}_{T+1})}{\exp\big(\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})^{\top}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{T+1})\big)}\middle|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
