<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.3" class="ltx_p">The two vectors <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> are the values with respect to the embeddings <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math> Points that are mapped to nearby points with respect to <math id="S5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> could be mapped to far away points with respect to <math id="S5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math> Thus, small changes in <math id="S5.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> can lead to a large change in the conditional expectation of <math id="S5.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}|\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1}|\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> such that we cannot guarantee any Hölder smoothness of the second factor in (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>). The first factor of (<a href="#S5.E28" title="In 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) does, however, not depend on the interplay between <math id="S5.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math> For the
direction of <math id="S5.p6.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}\mapsto\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\mapsto\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}|\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]</annotation></semantics></math> it is therefore possible to establish Hölder smoothness.
Consequently, one can show that the direction of <math id="S5.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to that of <math id="S5.p6.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}\mid\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{Y}_{T+1}\mid\mathbf{h},\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1}=\mathbf{z}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is close to <math id="S5.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}/\|\mathbf{h}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}/\|\mathbf{h}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this intuition
and properties of the von Mises–Fisher distribution, we present the following key lemma that in particular allows us later to construct a high-probability lower bound of <math id="S5.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of the lemma is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Directional Convergence of the Nadaraya–Watson Estimator ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconj26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconj26.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6</span></span><span id="Thmconj26.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconj26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconj26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose Assumption <a href="#Thmassumpt2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> holds. For sufficiently large <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and
for any <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\leq\log^{-1}(W\land T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\leq\log^{-1}(W\land T)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\frac{\delta_{2}}{\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}}\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})-\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T})\right\|^{2}_{2}\,\bigg|\,\mathbf{h}\right]=\tilde{O}\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\tfrac{1}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}+\kappa_{W}^{2}\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\left\|\frac{\delta_{2}}{\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}}\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})-\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T})\right\|^{2}_{2}\,\bigg|\,\mathbf{h}\right]=\tilde{O}\Big(\lambda^{2\beta_{2}}+\tfrac{1}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}+\kappa_{W}^{2}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj26.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj26.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, there exists a constant <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}\big[\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}&lt;c\,\big|\,\mathbf{h}\big]\lesssim\frac{1}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\big[\|\widehat{\mathbf{m}}_{\lambda}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\|_{2}&lt;c\,\big|\,\mathbf{h}\big]\lesssim\frac{1}{T\varphi_{W}(\lambda)^{r_{2}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconj26.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconj26.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All implicit constants and the constant <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> depend only on <math id="Thmconj26.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1},m_{2},r_{1},r_{2},\delta_{1},\delta_{2},\beta_{2},\|\mathbf{h}\|_{\mathcal{C}^{\beta_{2}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmrem1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmrem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The convergence rate in Lemma <a href="#Thmconj26" title="Lemma 6. ‣ 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> does not depend on <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> although the true probability model depends on <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In an additive regression setting <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{X}_{1,t})+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{X}_{2,t})+\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{X}_{1,t})+\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{X}_{2,t})+\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}(\epsilon_{t})=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}(\epsilon_{t})=0,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\ldots,T," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\ldots,T,</annotation></semantics></math> such a result can be easily obtained by considering the adjusted samples <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{2,t},\,Y_{t}-\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{X}_{1,t})\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{2,t},\,Y_{t}-\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{X}_{1,t})\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we argue that this approach cannot be extended to the
statistical model (<a href="#S5.E27" title="In 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) even when <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmrem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are known.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#Thmconj25" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#Thmconj26" title="Lemma 6. ‣ 5 Proof Sketch of the Upper Bound for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.EGx35.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.EGx35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Big(\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}\big(P+EX\big)\Big)_{:,T}\eqcolon\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\end{array}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≕</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mphantom></mphantom></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big(\bm{\mu}_{2}\circ\bm{\nu}_{1}\circ\bm{\mu}_{1}\big(P+EX\big)\Big)_{:,T}\eqcolon\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\left.\right.\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">satisfies <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{1}\approx\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{1}\approx\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{2}\mathbf{k}_{2}/\|\mathbf{k}_{2}\|_{2}\approx\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{2}\mathbf{k}_{2}/\|\mathbf{k}_{2}\|_{2}\approx\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{k}_{2}\|_{2}\geq c/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{k}_{2}\|_{2}\geq c/2</annotation></semantics></math> holds with high probability.
Using these results, we construct the second MLP layer <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mlp</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}_{2}\in\mathcal{MLP}(N,L_{\mathrm{mlp}},N_{\mathrm{mlp}},B)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\nu}_{2}\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\\
\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\left.\right.\end{array}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mphantom></mphantom></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\nu}_{2}\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{k}_{1}\\
\bullet_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\mathbf{k}_{2}\end{matrix}\right)\approx\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\\
\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\\
\end{matrix}\right)\begin{array}[]{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{1}}\\
\in\mathbb{R}^{m_{2}}\\
\left.\right.\end{array}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.3" class="ltx_p">See Lemma <a href="#Thmconj225" title="Lemma 25. ‣ E.2 Construction of multilayer perceptron layers ‣ Appendix E Construction of the Transformer ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a> for the detailed approximation error.
By considering the decoder matrix <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\coloneq E^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\coloneq E^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we finally get</p>
<table id="S5.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.EGx36.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.EGx36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\\
\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\end{array}\right)=\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},X)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋮</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D\left(\begin{matrix}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})\\
\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{0}_{N-m_{1}-m_{2}}\end{matrix}\right)=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\vdots\\
\bm{\phi}_{1}(\mathbf{e}_{W})^{\top}\mathbf{g}_{0}(\mathbf{Z}_{1,T+1})+\bm{\phi}_{2}(\mathbf{e}_{W})^{\top}\mathbf{h}(\mathbf{Z}_{2,T+1})\\
\end{array}\right)=\mathbf{f}_{0}(\mathbf{h},X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.4" class="ltx_p">See Section <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proofs for the Approximation Error ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for a formal proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmconj24" title="Theorem 4. ‣ 4.1 Proof of the risk bound ‣ 4 Risk bound ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is by now a substantial literature on the statistical learning theory of neural networks.
For DNNs, theoretical results have been developed for a wide range of statistical tasks, including nonparametric regression and classification, with convergence rates depending on smoothness, (intrinsic) dimension, or compositional structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>.
Statistical guarantees have also been obtained for other architectures.
CNNs have been analyzed under structural assumptions motivated by the translation-invariance of image data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
The statistical analysis of recurrent neural networks (RNNs) focuses on sequential data with temporal dependence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As transformers have become central to modern machine learning, recent studies started to investigate their approximation and representation capabilities.
Among them, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite> proved universal approximation results for continuous sequence-to-sequence functions and analyzed the roles of self-attention and feed-forward layers.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> showed that transformers can efficiently represent sparse functions of long input sequences, while
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>
identified tasks where transformers are more efficient than DNNs and RNNs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> showed that self-attention can approximate generalized ReLU-type operations, providing another route to universal approximation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent statistical papers provided a statistical analysis of transformers within the standard supervised learning framework.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> analyzed nonparametric binary classification with transformers under hierarchical composition structure, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> extended this result to overparametrized transformer classifiers trained by gradient descent.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> studied nonparametric regression with transformers and established convergence rates for sequence-to-sequence functions with infinite-dimensional inputs under suitable assumptions on the target function.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> analyzed transformers with ReLU self-attention for nonparametric regression and input data supported on a manifold, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> extended this analysis to noisy manifold settings.
Most recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> analyzed
transformers for nonparametric regression under Hölder smoothness, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> studied vision transformers with hierarchically compositional target functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another line of statistical research studies the theoretical properties of transformers for in-context learning in regression settings, showing that they can generalize to unseen tasks.
For instance,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> showed that linear-attention transformers can implement standard learning algorithms, including gradient descent, in context.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> analyzed transformers for in-context nonparametric regression and showed that pretraining can improve in-context estimation by encoding basis representations relevant to the target function class.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> studied in-context learning with transformers pretrained on mixtures of nonparametric regression tasks, establishing adaptation to task difficulty and robustness to certain forms of test-time distribution shift.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> connected transformers with the Nadaraya–Watson and local polynomial estimators for in-context nonparametric regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed N2F language model generates the next token from a latent context function and a finite token history.
Topic models have a rich statistical theory and can also be viewed as latent context models. Specifically, in the probabilistic latent semantic indexing (pLSI) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> one assumes that for each observed document there is a latent weight vector that determines the mixture weights over <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> topics (e.g. sport, politics, news, …). The word frequency in the document is then modeled as weighted sum over the word frequencies of the individual topics. The pLSI model is considerably simpler, as it only models the word frequencies but not the text as a sequence. The latent object is moreover a weight vector and not a function as in the proposed model in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Statistical modeling of next-token prediction ‣ Generalization Bounds for Transformer-Based Next-Token Prediction in a Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. There is an impressive line of existing statistical theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, but the assumptions, the mathematical analysis, and the theoretical results differ significantly from our work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A growing line of recent research applies Markov chain theory to study language models that generate token from a finite token history.
In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> analyzed estimation errors under the assumption that the training and test documents all share the same transition probabilities.
In contrast, in the proposed N2F language model each document has its own transition probabilities.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> proved that transformers can represent the <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator, and that the corresponding parameters form a stationary point of the population cross-entropy loss.
This analysis is different as it considers a single document, does not introduce an estimator, and relies on the population cross-entropy loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Estimating Markov transition matrices is a classical problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.
If the Markov chain of interest has only finitely many states, a natural estimator of the next-token distribution is given by the empirical frequency. The <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator is a special case of the empirical frequency under the implicit assumption that each document is generated according to a Markov chain of order <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
If applied to the language model setting, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> proved that if <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{(document length)}\geq\text{(vocabulary size)}^{r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(document length)</mtext><mo>≥</mo><msup><mtext>(vocabulary size)</mtext><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{(document length)}\geq\text{(vocabulary size)}^{r+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then an averaged version of an additively smoothed empirical frequency estimator is minimax optimal. This suggests that the <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-gram estimator is minimax rate-optimal. However, for text data the transition matrices are highly structured and should allow for faster estimation rates. Moreover,
<math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{(document length)}\ll\text{(vocabulary size)}^{r+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>(document length)</mtext><mo>≪</mo><msup><mtext>(vocabulary size)</mtext><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{(document length)}\ll\text{(vocabulary size)}^{r+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the more relevant regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The derived theory is also related to nonparametric estimation for Markov processes. Past research has for example focused on invariant density estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> and drift estimation for diffusion processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, with Nadaraya–Watson type estimators studied in e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are extremely grateful to Richard Samworth and Tianyi Ma for sharing their insights on the topic. All authors acknowledge support from ERC grant A2B (grant agreement no. 101124751). Parts of the research were carried out while the authors visited the Simons Institute in
Berkeley.</p>
</div>
</section>
